WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.228
Secure STL usage starts with using standard-library abstractions deliberately.

2
00:00:04.228 --> 00:00:13.530
Prefer containers, strings, views, spans, algorithms, and wrappers when they make ownership, bounds, and intent clearer than manual memory and ad hoc

3
00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:13.952
loops.

4
00:00:13.952 --> 00:00:19.872
Container choice should support the expected access pattern, lifetime, size behavior, and ownership model.

5
00:00:19.872 --> 00:00:22.409
Store values or smart pointers intentionally.

6
00:00:22.409 --> 00:00:27.482
Bound externally influenced growth before it affects memory, iteration, or lookup structures.

7
00:00:27.482 --> 00:00:29.596
Respect lifetime, bounds, and invalidation.

8
00:00:29.596 --> 00:00:32.979
Validate indexes, counts, offsets, and lengths before use.

9
00:00:32.979 --> 00:00:35.515
Do not confuse size with capacity.

10
00:00:35.515 --> 00:00:41.435
Refresh or discard iterators, references, pointers, and views after mutations that can invalidate them.

11
00:00:41.435 --> 00:00:51.582
Keep views and iterators review-sensitive. string_view and span are powerful because they preserve length and avoid copies, but they do not own data.

12
00:00:51.582 --> 00:00:55.810
They should not outlive or outlast mutation of their source.

13
00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.192
Finally, combine library use with testing and tooling.

14
00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:06.380
Use clear algorithms, consistent comparators, explicit synchronization for shared mutable containers, sanitizers, debug modes where available, static

15
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.071
analysis, and code review.

16
00:01:08.071 --> 00:01:13.568
Standard-library design reduces risk, but secure C++ still depends on disciplined use.

17
00:01:13.568 --> 00:01:15.682
The durable habit is explicitness.

18
00:01:15.682 --> 00:01:23.292
Choose the abstraction intentionally, document the assumptions that matter, and make failure, growth, lifetime, and invalidation behavior visible

19
00:01:23.292 --> 00:01:27.520
enough that the next maintainer can keep the code safe.
